From 4efba69f7757895822f4ff4a9eb990ae33cb40fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alessio Igor Bogani <alessio.bogani@elettra.eu>
Date: Mon, 4 May 2026 14:03:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] recog: don't gcc_unreachable in apply_to_rvalue_1's
 address re-canonicalizer

After insn_propagation::apply_to_rvalue_1 substitutes TO for FROM in an
rtx that lives under a MEM (mem_depth > 0) and TO is neither a REG nor
a CONSTANT, the function recursively invokes apply_to_rvalue on *loc to
canonicalize the new address.  Upstream asserts that this recursion can
never fail (gcc_unreachable).

That assumption breaks on STRICT_ALIGNMENT targets such as
powerpc-spe (linuxspe.h sets MASK_STRICT_ALIGN by default).  When the
substituted form contains a SUBREG of an under-aligned MEM that
changes size between FP modes -- e.g. (subreg:DF (mem:TF [...]) 0)
where the MEM is 4-byte aligned -- validate_subreg rejects the SUBREG
post-LRA (FP<->FP size changes are only allowed during LRA), so
apply_to_rvalue_1's SUBREG case returns false with failure_reason
"would create an invalid subreg".  The recursion thus legitimately
returns false, and the gcc_unreachable trips.

We see this concretely as an ICE during the late_combine RTL pass when
cross-compiling C++ code with the powerpcspe backend (-mcpu=8548
-mabi=spe -mspe -mfloat-gprs=double).

Failing the recursion gracefully instead of asserting lets the caller
chain cancel the speculative substitution and move on -- exactly the
intended fall-back path for "this combine would not validate".  This
matches the broader contract of insn_propagation, which is supposed to
back out cleanly on any validation failure.

No reproducer included in tree because triggering the path requires a
specific late_combine attempt on TFmode under SPE; verified that the
existing TFmode test battery still produces the expected SPE codegen
(evldd / evstdd / efd*) at -O0 / -O2 / -O3 / -Os.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 gcc/recog.cc | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/gcc/recog.cc b/gcc/recog.cc
index edf22cba807..e2f91c2f119 100644
--- a/gcc/recog.cc
+++ b/gcc/recog.cc
@@ -1109,25 +1109,33 @@ insn_propagation::apply_to_rvalue_1 (rtx *loc)
 	}
 
       if (should_unshare)
 	validate_unshare_change (insn, loc, newval, 1);
       else
 	validate_change (insn, loc, newval, 1);
       if (mem_depth && !REG_P (newval) && !CONSTANT_P (newval))
 	{
 	  /* We're substituting into an address, but TO will have the
 	     form expected outside an address.  Canonicalize it if
-	     necessary.  */
+	     necessary.
+
+	     Most failures here are upstream gcc_unreachable, but on
+	     STRICT_ALIGNMENT targets such as powerpc-spe the
+	     post-substitution form can legitimately contain a SUBREG
+	     that validate_subreg rejects (e.g. an FP-mode size change
+	     of an under-aligned MEM).  Returning false in that case
+	     lets the caller cancel this substitution and try another
+	     combine, which is what we want.  */
 	  insn_propagation subprop (insn);
 	  subprop.mem_depth += 1;
 	  if (!subprop.apply_to_rvalue (loc))
-	    gcc_unreachable ();
+	    return false;
 	  if (should_unshare
 	      && num_validated_changes () != old_num_changes + 1)
 	    {
 	      /* TO is owned by someone else, so create a copy and
 		 return TO to its original form.  */
 	      newval = copy_rtx (*loc);
 	      cancel_changes (old_num_changes);
 	      validate_change (insn, loc, newval, 1);
 	    }
 	}
